Implement a gpt LBA-to-PBA mapper, as a pass-through BlockDev.

Refactor test_fat32 a bit so it can be built for firmware (with a
suitable block device, like an SD or CF card and driver) and run on a
target platform.

Add locking, just a simple variety that only allows one API function
to be in flight at any one time.  Create a skeleton that does nothing
which can be used for non-MT platforms that don't need it, and as a
template for those that do.

Implement pthread-based locking for dev purposes.  Extend test_fat32
to aggressively test it.

Investigate cluster loading; when loading a sector ask the cache to
fill the entire cluster.  Add a fill() method to BlockDev for this.

Add a no-cache hint to read/write, or an alternate version.  For
example, when flushing large amounts of data that has been collected
and then never using it again this would otherwise cause the entire
sector cache to be evicted.  The flag would signify that the caller
isn't expected to touch this data again anytime soon, if ever.

When adding GPT partition mapping (LBA to PBA) verify that the LBA is
inside the bounds and not in the hidden/reserved prologue.  It might
also be possible to refuse to write to the BPB or FSINFO without using
a specific function to do so, to prevent accidental corruption.  Either
way, a write to LBA 0 or such should cause an error code that tells
the caller there is corruption and that fsck should be run.

Verify that the partition is between 32MB and 32TB.  Especially the
lower limit is a hard stop for FAT32.

Extend Stat to include attributes and timestamps.

Add fsck repair stats to the report.

The data structures used all have a little-endian dependency. Is this
even worth the bother these days?
